+### 4.17 The replayer
 
-The **replayer** is a tile unit that regenerates the tile's share of a proof's execution trace from the run log the tracer engine writes, for the units that consume it. Its design is being revised and is **to be specified with the replayer**. v1.1 fixes only its **hooks** into the rest of the tile, so that the link word, the packet modes, the registers and the wake can be settled now.
+*No figures yet; the figure set gains a replayer part (handoff in `CHANGES-v1.1.md`).*
 
-| hook | where | reserved for |
+The **replayer** is a tile unit that regenerates the tile's share of a proof's execution trace, 2¹⁶ rows of Jolt's expanded trace, from the run log the tracer engine writes, and streams the rows to the unit that consumes them (the materializer, outside this specification). It recomputes every row it can with a small ALU and takes from the log only the words it cannot compute. This section specifies it in full; it follows the owner's design notes *Replayer and mesh v1.1* (updated 2026-10-23) and their shared-resource allocation table.
+
+#### 4.17.1 Overview
+
+**What a pass is.** A replay start (`TILE_PULSE` [2], 4.17.11) loads the tile's checkpoint and runs exactly **65,536 rows**, then stops; that is one **pass**. The replayer runs many passes per proof, once for every sumcheck stage that needs the trace (46 per Akita proof in the repo's kernel scratchpad plan [estimate]), and every pass starts from the same checkpoint, because replay never writes it.
+
+**The parts.**
+
+| part | size | role | section |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| code queue | 4 KB, 1,024 × 32 bits | the opcode stream: one word per native instruction | 4.17.8 |
+| expansion queue | 4 KB, 256 × 128 bits | expansion words the controller supplies (inlines, system instructions) | 4.17.8 |
+| value queue | 8 KB, 1,024 × 64 bits | the memory log | 4.17.8 |
+| decode table | 4 KB, 2K × 16 bits | instruction → template | 4.17.5 |
+| ROM | 8 KB, 512 × 128 bits, dual-ported | the templates: one expansion word per row | 4.17.6 |
+| register image, state word | 1 KB + 16 B | the checkpoint | 4.17.2 |
+| working register file | 1 KB, 128 × 64 bits, true dual-port | the registers a pass reads and writes | 4.17.7 |
+| ALU, comparator, two adders | — | row results, branch outcomes, memory address, pc + imm | 4.17.7 |
+| output queue | 32 rows of 80 B | rows waiting for the consumer | 4.17.12 |
+
+**Its interfaces.** Every replayer memory and queue is written by **mode 8** (4.17.10). The controller starts a pass with `TILE_PULSE` [2], follows it with `TILE_STATUS` [3] and [4], serves it through three registers (`REPLAYER_OPCODE`, `REPLAYER_STATUS`, `DMA_REPLAYER_REMAINING`, 8.11) and is woken by its five events in the shared `WAKE_EVENTS` register (bits [20:16], 4.17.9). Its errors are `TILE_CONDITIONS` [13:11] (4.17.11).
+
+**Rate.** A row takes a fixed 3 cycles, or 5 for a multiply (4.17.7): about 3.3 cycles per row with about 10 % multiply rows [estimate; to be measured, item 37], so a pass is about 216K cycles plus about 1.3K cycles of controller-fed expansion stalls, about 0.22 ms at 1 GHz with everything local [estimate]. The consuming kernels likely need 3–6 cycles per row anyway [estimate].
+
+**The reference.** The rows must match Jolt's bit for bit. The reference is the jolt-cpp tracer copy on the `tracer-standalone` branch of `jolt-asic-planning` (PR #1, commit `4f36b3d`); citations in this section are to files under its `tracer/src/tracer/`. Three of its functions are normative: `expand_virt_seq` (`isa/virtual.cpp:132`) for what an instruction expands to, `execute_instruction` (`trace_owner.cpp:145`) for what a row computes, and `expand_compressed_chunk` (`trace_owner.cpp:868`) for what a row records. Where this section departs from them (log-fed values, unchecked assertions), it says so.
+
+**Status.** The design notes record most of this design as candidate and some of it as decided (the run log, 2026-10-22 and 2026-10-23; the fixed row schedules, every instruction a template, the queues as true FIFOs and the resident region, all 2026-10-23; the allocation table, confirmed 2026-10-23). This section states the notes' design unmarked, as the design to build; its own additions are marked *(Proposed)* and gathered in section 13 (items 33–59), and so are the details the owner accepted as proposals on 2026-10-23. Facts about Jolt's expansions are cited to the tracer; the template enumeration that would confirm them across real programs is open (item 34).
+
+#### 4.17.2 The run log and the checkpoint
+
+The tracer engine writes, per tile, two streams and a checkpoint. They live in the tile's 512 KB resident replay region of big SRAM (4.17.13) and reach the replayer through its queues.
+
+| stream | granularity | contents | per tile |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **opcode** | one 32-bit word per native instruction, held while its expansion runs | the RISC-V instruction; a compressed one is stored decompressed with bit 0 cleared as its flag | about 43K words (175 KB) at 1.5 rows per native instruction; 256 KB at most [estimate] |
+| **memory log** | one 64-bit word per row that needs one, in row order | below | 0.28–0.32 words per row on average, up to 0.833 in the densest windows: about 160 KB on average, 437 KB at most [meas: design notes] |
+
+Every stored instruction has bit 1 set, so **bits [1:0] = 00 mark a padding word** (below) and bits [1:0] = 10 a compressed instruction.
+
+**Which rows take a log word.** At most one per row, in row order:
+- **LD:** the value loaded.
+- **SD:** the memory's pre-value; the post-value is rs2's value.
+- **Advice:** `VirtualAdvice` and the DIV/REM advice rows (4.17.7, class L): the advice value.
+- **A control transfer:** a row with `next_from_value` set (4.17.3) whose new pc differs from its pc (4.17.7): the next `instr_idx`.
+
+The first three are the rows whose expansion word has `from_log` set (4.17.3).
+
+The measured densities (words per row, LD / SD / advice / non-sequential) are 0.170 / 0.115 / 0.0001 / 0.030 on EVM Zone (total 0.315) and 0.147 / 0.090 / 0.0002 / 0.039 on Cyclone (total 0.277); per 64K-row window the median is 0.29–0.32 and the maximum 0.833 on both, and the worst sliding 512-, 1,024- and 4,096-row spans are all about 0.835, so dense stretches are sustained, not bursts [meas: design notes]. A replay pass reads 9.1–11.1 MB of log per chip on EVM Zone and 4.0–15.4 MB on Cyclone [meas: design notes].
+
+**A requirement on our tracer engine.** The run log differs from jolt-cpp's memory log on purpose. jolt-cpp logs only LD, SD and `VirtualAdvice` (`trace_owner.cpp:822–855`): it computes the DIV/REM advice in its interpreter (`trace_owner.cpp:545–630`) and looks a jump's next index up in a pc-to-index table (`finish_instruction`, `trace_owner.cpp:111–126`). The replayer has no divider and no such table, so **the tracer engine must write the DIV/REM advice and the next `instr_idx` of every control transfer into the run log**, in row order with the other log words (item 33). The validation harness (item 58) proves the format, the tracer engine's extra words and the replayer together.
+
+**The checkpoint.** Per tile, loaded by mode 8 (4.17.10):
+- the **register image**: all 128 registers, as 64 words of 128 bits; word *i* holds register 2*i* in bits [63:0] and 2*i* + 1 in [127:64];
+- the **state word** (128 bits): pc [63:0], `instr_idx` [95:64], next offset [111:96] (the rows of the first instruction's expansion that the previous tile already ran), spare [127:112].
+
+Sending all 128 registers avoids special-casing a mid-sequence start, at 1 KB per tile. **A tile that starts inside an expansion** has that instruction as its first opcode-stream entry, and the next offset says where in the expansion to begin. jolt-cpp's chunked replay instead rewinds to the start of the expansion when inline scratch registers are live (`trace_owner.cpp:722–739`); the full image makes that unnecessary. **Register 0** of the image is ignored: the working file's register 0 always reads 0 *(Proposed, item 41)*.
+
+**Re-runs.** The start copies the image into the working register file (64 cycles, two registers per cycle) and the state word into working copies of pc, `instr_idx` and the offset. Replay never writes the image or the state word, so every pass starts from the same point.
+
+**Inline scratch.** Registers 48–127 hold an expansion's scratch values and are 0 between expansions (`isa/virtual.hpp:25–27`). Inline templates end with explicit `ADDI vN, x0, 0` rows that zero each scratch register they used (for example the end of `inlines/blake3.inc`), so the replayer needs no state for them. jolt-cpp also zeroes them defensively at chunk starts (`normalize_inline_vregs`, `trace_owner.cpp:128`); with the full register image the replayer doesn't need to.
+
+**End of trace.** The trace is padded to 2²⁹ rows after the program ends. The program's last real row jumps to pc 0: a JAL with immediate 0 sends pc to 0 (`trace_owner.cpp:281`), and its log word sets `instr_idx` to 0. The tracer writes the padding as real opcode entries: one opcode word 0x00000000 per padding row, with no log words. The padding word maps to the ROM's padding template (4.17.6), an all-zero NOP row with `hold` set, so pc and `instr_idx` stay 0, as in the tracer's padding, where every padded row is zero and decodes as the zero-filled NOP at bytecode index 0 (`trace_owner.hpp:160–163`; real instructions start at index 1, `isa/virtual.cpp:1359`).
+
+#### 4.17.3 The expansion word and the operand codes
+
+Every row is described by one **128-bit expansion word**, in place of the tracer's 48-byte `VirtualInstruction` (`isa/virtual.hpp:89–126`). The same format serves the ROM and the words the controller supplies.
+
+| bits | field | contents |
+|---|---|---|
+| [7:0] | `opcode` | the row opcode, 0–167, numbered in the tracer's order (`isa/riscv.inc`, enum `OpCode`, `isa/riscv.hpp:17`); 4.17.7 lists the ones that occur in rows |
+| [15:8] | `rd` | operand code (below) |
+| [23:16] | `rs1` | operand code |
+| [31:24] | `rs2` | operand code |
+| [43:32] | `seq_remaining` | rows left in the expansion after this one; 0 on its last row. At most 3,120 (`KECCAK256_ABSORB_PERMUTE` expands to 3,121 rows, `isa/virtual.cpp:1310`) |
+| [44] | `next_from_value` | this row can change control flow: when it does (4.17.7), the next `instr_idx` comes from the log |
+| [45] | `hold` | pc and `instr_idx` don't advance after this row (padding) |
+| [47:46] | `rd0` | the rd = x0 policy of the template (4.17.4): 0 none, 1 NOP (the NOP row is virtual), 2 remap, 3 NOP (the NOP row is not virtual: the source is ADDI). Read from a template's first word only |
+| [50:48] | `format` | the source instruction's immediate format, for code 132: 0 R, 1 I, 2 S, 3 B, 4 U, 5 J; 6 and 7 reserved |
+| [51] | `virt` | the row's opcode differs from the source instruction's *(Proposed, item 35)* |
+| [52] | `imm0_pc0` | Jolt's program-end convention: if this row is a JAL whose immediate is 0, its new pc is 0 (4.17.7) *(Proposed, item 56)* |
+| [53] | `report_c` | report `is_compressed` on this row when the instruction is compressed (4.17.12) *(Proposed, item 56)* |
+| [54] | `from_log` | the row consumes one log word: rd's value, or an SD row's memory pre-value (4.17.7) *(Proposed, item 56)* |
+| [63:55] | spare | 9 bits, 0 |
+| [127:64] | `const` | a constant immediate (code 128) |
+
+**`virt`** *(Proposed, item 35)*. Jolt marks a row virtual when its expansion has more than one row **or its opcode differs from the source instruction's** (`isa/virtual.cpp:183`). The second half is not derivable in hardware, since the replayer never sees the source instruction as a row opcode: SLLI becomes one `VirtualMULI` row and is virtual, while ADD stays one ADD row and is not. The template builder therefore sets `virt` on every word whose opcode isn't the source's, and the replayer derives the rest (below).
+
+`seq_remaining` = 0 marks the end of an expansion; there is no separate end bit. The per-instruction fields (`rd0`, `format`, `next_from_value`) live in the ROM words, not the decode table, because every instruction is a template (4.17.5).
+
+**Operand codes.** Each of `rd`, `rs1` and `rs2` names where the slot's value comes from:
+
+| code | meaning | slot kind |
+|---|---|---|
+| 0–127 | register: x0–x31, then virtual registers 32–127 | register |
+| 128 | `const` (also the zero placeholder, with `const` = 0) | immediate |
+| 129, 130, 131 | the source instruction's rd, rs1, rs2 field (bits [11:7], [19:15], [24:20]) | register |
+| 132 | the source instruction's immediate, decoded by `format` and sign-extended to 64 bits (`isa/decoder.cpp:74–212`); 0 for format R | immediate |
+| 133 | `mask_trailing_zeros(imm & 0x3f)`: ones in bits [63:*s*], *s* = `imm & 0x3f` (SRLI, SRAI; `isa/virtual.cpp:565–573`, `../utils/math.hpp:134`) | immediate |
+| 134 | `1 << (imm & 0x3f)` (SLLI → `VirtualMULI`; `isa/virtual.cpp:596–600`) | immediate |
+| 135 | `1 << (imm & 0x1f)`, as a 32-bit value (SLLIW → `VirtualMULIW`; `isa/virtual.cpp:616–620`) | immediate |
+| 136 | `(1 << 32) − (1 << (imm & 0x1f))`, the 32-bit window bitmask (SRLIW, SRAIW; `isa/virtual.cpp:575–579`, `629–633`) | immediate |
+| 137–144 | temporary *k* = code − 137, resolved as register 40 + *k* + offset (4.17.4) | register |
+| 145–255 | reserved: an invalid expansion (4.17.11) | — |
+
+In codes 133–136, `imm` is the source instruction's immediate, the same value as code 132; the masks make the shift-immediate formats' funct bits irrelevant (the tracer decodes shift immediates with their own shamt format, `isa/decoder.cpp:98`, and only these codes ever use them). Templates never contain literal registers 40–47; they use the temporary codes.
+
+**The slot kind is committed.** A register slot reads its register; an immediate slot carries its value, and Jolt records which slots are immediates (`rd_is_imm`, `rs1_is_imm`, `rs2_is_imm`, `isa/virtual.hpp:100–102`). So code 0 (x0) and code 128 with `const` = 0 differ: a zero placeholder must not replay as x0.
+
+**The row's immediate** is the sum of its immediate slots' values, as Jolt computes it (`trace_owner.cpp:900`); in every template the tracer emits, at most one immediate slot is non-zero (to be confirmed by the enumeration, item 34).
+
+**Derived, not stored:**
+- `is_first`: the row is the first of its expansion and the expansion is virtual (`isa/virtual.cpp:186`);
+- `is_virtual`: `virt` is set, or the expansion has more than one row (`seq_remaining` > 0, or the row is not the first); for the shared NOP row, `rd0` = 1 (4.17.4);
+- `pc_increment`: 0 unless `seq_remaining` = 0, then 2 for a compressed instruction (opcode word bit 0 = 0) and 4 otherwise; only an expansion's last row carries it (`isa/virtual.cpp:1416`);
+- `is_compressed`: the instruction is compressed and the word has `report_c` set. Jolt reports it only where `pc_increment` = 2 (`trace_owner.hpp:184`, `trace_owner.cpp:899`), that is on an expansion's last row, so the template builder sets `report_c` on last words only;
+- `risc_pc`: the pc register, the same for every row of an expansion (`isa/virtual.cpp:1413–1415`).
+
+**Jolt's conventions are data where they can be** *(Proposed, item 56)*. Each place where the replayer must copy a Jolt convention rather than plain RISC-V is a flag in the expansion word or a code in it, set by whoever builds the tables, so a change in Jolt means new tables rather than new silicon: `virt` (which rows Jolt calls virtual), `rd0` 1 and 3 (whether the rd = x0 NOP row is virtual), `imm0_pc0` (JAL to immediate 0 ends the program), `report_c` (which rows carry `is_compressed`), `from_log` (which rows take a log word), and the advice rows' operands (4.17.7). What stays in logic is listed in 4.17.7 and item 57.
+
+#### 4.17.4 The rd = x0 policy and the temporary offset
+
+Jolt rewrites an instruction whose rd is x0 before expanding it (`isa/virtual.cpp:307–319`):
+- with **no side effect beyond writing rd**, it becomes one NOP row, `ADDI x0, x0, 0` (`isa/virtual.cpp:307–311`);
+- with **side effects**, rd is remapped to a throwaway virtual register, v40, and the template's own temporaries then start at v41 (`isa/virtual.cpp:315–319`; temporaries are allocated upward from 40, `isa/virtual.cpp:140`, `148–153`);
+- **a few instructions handle rd = x0 themselves** and are expanded unchanged (`rd0_is_handled_internally`, `isa/virtual.cpp:57–102`).
+
+The replayer reproduces this with the template's `rd0` policy and a one-bit **offset**.
+
+**The rule.** For one expansion, offset = 1 when the source instruction's rd field (bits [11:7]) is 0 and the template's `rd0` is remap; otherwise 0. Then:
+- temporary *k* (codes 137–144) resolves to register 40 + *k* + offset;
+- under remap, the source rd (code 129) resolves to register 40.
+
+Under a **NOP** policy with rd = x0, the instruction becomes the one shared NOP row (4.17.6) in place of its template. The tracer marks that row virtual unless the source instruction is ADDI itself (`isa/virtual.cpp:183`), so the policy says which: `rd0` = 1 makes the NOP row virtual (and so `is_first`), and `rd0` = 3, used only by ADDI's template, makes it not virtual *(Proposed, item 35)*. The row's virtual-ness is data, not an opcode compare.
+
+**Example.** `lb a5, 4(a0)` against `lb x0, 4(a0)` (`expand_load`, `isa/virtual.cpp:237–250`):
+
+| row | normal (offset 0) | remapped (offset 1) |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | `VirtualAlignAddr v41, a0, 4` | `VirtualAlignAddr v42, a0, 4` |
+| 2 | `LD v41, v41, 0` | `LD v42, v42, 0` |
+| 3 | `VirtualWindowMaskB v40, a0, 4` | `VirtualWindowMaskB v41, a0, 4` |
+| 4 | `VirtualPextSigned a5, v41, v40` | `VirtualPextSigned v40, v42, v41` |
+
+**Which instructions remap**: those in `has_side_effects_beyond_rd` and not in `rd0_is_handled_internally` (`isa/virtual.cpp:19–55`, `57–102`):
+
+| instructions | served by | effect |
+|---|---|---|
+| JAL, JALR, LD, `VirtualHostIO` | one-word ROM templates | rd becomes 40; e.g. `ret` becomes `JALR v40, ra, 0` |
+| LB, LBU, LH, LHU, LW, LWU | ROM templates | rd becomes 40, temporaries + 1 |
+| the 18 AMOs | ROM templates | rd becomes 40, temporaries + 1; no stored variants |
+
+LR/SC, CSRRW, CSRRS, ECALL, EBREAK, MRET and the inlines are exempt (`rd0` = 0), as is every instruction whose rd field is an immediate (stores, branches, `VirtualAssertEQ`, FENCE; `isa/virtual.cpp:104–106`). JAL, JALR and `VirtualHostIO` also allocate their own throwaway register when rd is x0 (`isa/virtual.cpp:1195–1226`); after the generic remap that case never arises, and both paths give rd = 40, so one-word remap templates reproduce them. With the decode table's AMO index (4.17.5), every atomic reaches its own template and the offset handles the remap in hardware.
+
+**The temporaries stay in 40–47.** The tracer asserts that no expansion, remap included, allocates beyond register 47 (`isa/virtual.cpp:148–153`), so every correct template keeps 40 + *k* + offset ≤ 47. The replayer doesn't check it *(Proposed, item 36)*.
+
+**Mid-sequence start.** The offset is recomputed from the opcode-stream entry's rd field and the `rd0` of the template's **first word**, ROM[start], not ROM[start + offset]. Controller-fed expansions (inlines and system instructions) are all exempt, so their offset is always 0.
+
+#### 4.17.5 The decode table and its index
+
+**Every instruction is a template.** A pass-through instruction is a one-word template whose operands are codes 129–132 (copy the source instruction's registers or immediate), so there is one datapath: every row comes from an expansion word.
+
+**The decode table** is 2K × 16 bits (4 KB), single-ported. Its **11-bit index** is formed by a small hardware decoder from the opcode-stream word *w* (bit 0, the compressed flag, plays no part):
+
+| *w*[6:0] | index[10] | index[9:0] |
+|---|---|---|
+| any other opcode | 0 | {*w*[6:2], *w*[14:12] (funct3), *w*[30], *w*[25]} |
+| 0101111 (AMO) | 1 | {00, 00, *w*[12] (W/D), *w*[31:27] (funct5)}: separates LR, SC and every AMO |
+| 0001011 (custom-0, the inlines) | 1 | {01, *w*[29:25] (funct7[4:0]), *w*[14:12] (funct3)}: one entry per inline |
+| 1110011 (SYSTEM) | 1 | {10, 0000, *sel*}: *sel* = funct3 when funct3 ≠ 0 (the CSR instructions); when funct3 = 0, *sel* = 8 for ECALL (*w* = 0x00000073), 9 for EBREAK (0x00100073), 10 for MRET (0x30200073), 11 otherwise |
+
+*(Proposed, item 38: the special groups' bit positions; the notes fix only which instruction bits each group uses.)* Index[9:8] = 11 with index[10] = 1 is never formed. The general group's entries for the three special opcodes are never read.
+
+The jolt instruction encodings it must separate (`isa/decoder.cpp`): the inlines are custom-0 with funct7 0x00–0x05 and funct3 0–7 (`isa/decoder.cpp:1210–1249`); custom-2 (0x5B) carries `VirtualRev8W` (funct3 0, funct7 0x05), `VirtualAssertEQ` (funct3 1) and `VirtualHostIO` (funct3 2) (`isa/decoder.cpp:1251–1264`), which the general group separates by funct3; MRET is recognised by its exact encoding (`isa/decoder.cpp:603–606`).
+
+**The entry** uses 11 of 16 bits:
+
+| bits | field |
+|---|---|
+| [1:0] | `class`: 0 ROM template, 1 controller (inline), 2 controller (system), 3 reserved (an invalid expansion) |
+| [10:2] | `start`: the template's first ROM word |
+| [15:11] | spare |
+
+There is no length field: each ROM word's `seq_remaining` marks the template's end, including on a mid-sequence start (start + offset). **The padding word** (bits [1:0] = 00) is caught before the lookup and goes to the padding template (4.17.6).
+
+**The table builder's rules** (software contract): every entry is written, because the table isn't reset; unused entries are controller class, so an unknown instruction becomes a question for software rather than an error; and an instruction whose index bits include immediate bits (the U, J and most I formats) has its entry **replicated** across every index those bits can form. The table is per program, which also absorbs program-level choices: under the legacy raw-ECALL host-I/O encoding, the tracer rewrites ECALL as `VirtualHostIO x0, x0, 0` (`host_io_encoding.cpp:79–92`), so ECALL's entry points at a one-word `VirtualHostIO` remap template instead of the controller.
+
+#### 4.17.6 The ROM and its contents
+
+**The ROM is 8 KB, 512 × 128-bit words, dual-ported,** loaded by mode 8 (4.17.10); one port serves the replayer's reads and the other mode-8 writes, which it accepts even while a pass runs. It is a RAM; "ROM" says that replay never writes it. It holds every template the hardware serves:
+
+| content | words |
+|---|---|
+| ROM words 0 and 1: the **padding** row (word 0: opcode NOP, every operand code 0, `seq_remaining` 0, `hold` 1) and the **shared NOP** row (word 1: `ADDI x0, x0, 0`, i.e. opcode ADDI, `rd` 0, `rs1` 0, `rs2` 128 with `const` 0, `report_c` 1, since it is its instruction's only row) *(Proposed, item 39: their fixed places)* | 2 |
+| emulation sequences: shifts, sub-word loads and stores, the M extension (`isa/virtual.cpp:321–640`) | about 172 |
+| pass-through instructions, one word each | about 45 |
+| atomics: LR.D 5, LR.W 9, SC.D 12, SC.W 26, the 18 AMOs 237 (`isa/virtual.cpp:1287–1306`) | 289 |
+| **total** | **about 508 of 512** |
+
+The fit is tight; reusing identical words or a slightly larger ROM restores headroom (item 40). rd = x0 remaps need no variant words: the offset rule applies in hardware. With the atomics in the ROM, the controller serves only the inlines and the system instructions.
+
+**How a template is read.** A ROM template is consecutive words from `start` (from `start` + offset on a mid-sequence start), each word one row, until the word with `seq_remaining` = 0. A template that would run past word 511 (a word at 511 with `seq_remaining` > 0, or `start` + offset > 511) is an invalid expansion (4.17.11).
+
+**Loading.** The table and ROM load through mode 8, broadcast by C&C at boot (4.17.10).
+
+#### 4.17.7 Executing a row: the register file, the ALU and the schedules
+
+**Per row** the replayer:
+1. takes the current native instruction from the code queue and finds its row: the next ROM word of its template, or the next word of the expansion queue for a controller-fed instruction;
+2. resolves the row's operand codes (4.17.3, 4.17.4) and reads rs1, rs2 and rd_prev from the register file;
+3. computes the result with the ALU, or takes it from the log (class L, below); writes rd; forms the memory address as rs1 + immediate;
+4. pushes the row record into the output queue (4.17.12), and advances pc and `instr_idx` (below).
+
+An instruction's opcode word leaves the code queue when its expansion's last row (`seq_remaining` = 0) completes. Fetch and decode for the next row overlap the current row; only execution is strictly sequential *(Proposed, item 42)*.
+
+**The register file** is 128 × 64 bits (1 KB), true dual-port, and holds the working copy of all 128 registers. **Register 0 reads 0 and writes to it are dropped**, as the tracer's `save_rd` drops them (`trace_owner.cpp:85–93`). It is not addressable by mode 8; each start fills it from the image.
+
+**What a row computes** is what `execute_instruction` (`trace_owner.cpp:145–669`) computes for it, reading each slot's value as `read_reg_vals` does (a register slot reads its register, an immediate slot carries its value, `trace_owner.cpp:78–83`), with four departures:
+- **class L rows take the log word** (below) instead of reading memory or computing advice;
+- **assertions are not evaluated:** the `VirtualAssert…` rows write nothing, and the tracer aborts on a failed assertion while tracing (`release_assert`, e.g. `trace_owner.cpp:334–351`), so a traced program never contains one that fails;
+- **`VirtualPext` and `VirtualPextSigned`** need only contiguous masks, (rs1 & m) >> tz(m) and its sign-extended form: every mask they receive comes from a `VirtualWindowMask…` row (`expand_load`, `isa/virtual.cpp:237–250`) *(Proposed, item 43)*;
+- **control flow** follows the pc rules below, with the next `instr_idx` from the log rather than from a pc-to-index table.
+
+**Row classes and their schedules.** Of the tracer's 168 opcodes, 91 occur in Jolt's rows; 74 occur only as source instructions, which their templates replace, and 3 never occur (`VirtualMove` 126, `VirtualAdviceRemU` 161, `VirtualAdviceRemUW` 165) [derived from `isa/virtual.cpp` and `inlines/`; the enumeration, item 34, confirms it]. Codes are the tracer's (`isa/riscv.inc`):
+
+| class | cycles | opcodes (code) |
+|---|---|---|
+| **L**, log | 3 | LD 54 (rd = the log word), SD 53 (the log word is the memory pre-value; rd is an immediate slot), `VirtualAdvice` 122 (rd = the log word), and the DIV/REM advice opcodes `VirtualAdviceDivU` 160, `VirtualAdviceDiv` 162, `VirtualAdviceRem` 163, `VirtualAdviceDivUW` 164, `VirtualAdviceDivW` 166, `VirtualAdviceRemW` 167, which the templates write as `VirtualAdvice` (below) |
+| **M**, multiply | 5 | MUL 43 and `VirtualMULI` 114 (low 64 bits of the product), MULHU 45 (high 64 bits, unsigned), MULW 65 and `VirtualMULIW` 115 (low 32 bits, sign-extended) (`trace_owner.cpp:213–231`) |
+| **S**, simple | 3 | every other row opcode: NOP 0, ADD 1, ADDI 2, SUB 3, XOR 4, XORI 5, OR 6, ORI 7, AND 8, ANDI 9, SLT 16, SLTI 17, SLTU 18, SLTIU 19, BEQ 28, BNE 29, BLT 30, BGE 31, BLTU 32, BGEU 33, JAL 34, JALR 35, AUIPC 36, LUI 37, FENCE 52, SUBW 62, ADDW 63, ADDIW 64, and the virtual opcodes 107–113, 116–121, 123–125, 127–159 |
+| source only | — | the shifts SLL–SRAI 10–15 and SLLW–SRAIW 56–61, the sub-word loads and stores 20–27 and LWU 55, ECALL–MRET 38–42, MULH 44, MULHSU 46, DIV–REMU 47–50, UNIMP 51, DIVW–REMUW 66–69, LR/SC and the AMOs 70–91, the inlines 92–106 |
+
+*(Proposed, item 37: the class of every opcode; the notes settle only the principle.)* The class L rows are exactly those whose word has `from_log` set (4.17.3): the flag, not the opcode, decides that a row takes a log word. The replayer **executes 85 opcodes**: the S and M classes, LD, SD and `VirtualAdvice`. A word with any other opcode is an invalid expansion *(Proposed, item 36)*.
+
+**Advice rows are data** *(Proposed, item 56)*. Jolt reports every advice row as `VirtualAdvice` with rs1, rs2 and the immediate all 0 (`trace_owner.cpp:912–917`), though the tracer's DIV/REM advice rows name real source registers (`isa/virtual.cpp:331`, for example) so that its interpreter can compute them. The replayer takes the value from the log, so the template builder writes those rows as `VirtualAdvice rd, x0, x0` with `from_log` set: the record then comes out exactly as Jolt reports it, with no masking logic. (The inlines' own advice rows are already `VirtualAdvice` with two zero immediates, `isa/virtual.cpp:216–218`, and are recorded the same way.)
+
+**The multiplier needs no signed high half.** MULH and MULHSU never reach a row: the tracer expands them through MULHU and MUL (`isa/virtual.cpp:485–503`). So the multiplier produces the low product, the unsigned high product and the low 32 bits sign-extended; the sign corrections the notes provide for MULH are not needed *(Proposed, item 37)*. `VirtualAssertMulUNoOverflow` multiplies only to check, so it is class S.
+
+**The fixed row schedules** (decided 2026-10-23). Rows are strictly sequential, never overlapped, so there are no hazards and no bypass. The register file is true dual-port; every read of a row happens before its write, so a row whose rs1 or rs2 is also its rd reads the old value, as the tracer does (`trace_owner.cpp:903–908`).
+
+| row kind | cycles | schedule |
+|---|---|---|
+| simple (class S) and log rows (class L) | 3 | 1: read rs1 and rs2. 2: compute (or take the log word), read rd_prev. 3: write rd |
+| multiply (class M) | 5 | 1: read rs1 and rs2. 2: four 16 × 64 partial products, read rd_prev. 3–4: carry-save and final add into the 128-bit product. 5: write rd (low or high half, or the low word sign-extended) |
+
+The multiplier is four 16 × 64 multipliers in parallel, a full 64 × 64 array, which is cheap in area. There is no divider: Jolt's DIV and REM use advice plus checks, and the advice comes from the log. **Stalls** (an empty code, expansion or value queue, a full output queue) delay a row's start; they never change its schedule.
+
+**Semantics that matter for exactness** (from `execute_instruction`): AUIPC adds the source instruction's pc (`trace_owner.cpp:156–160`); JAL and JALR link pc + `pc_increment`, which is 2 or 4 because a JAL or JALR row is always its expansion's last row (`trace_owner.cpp:277–291`); `VirtualHostIO` writes rd's own value back (`trace_owner.cpp:294–299`); the W forms sign-extend their low 32 bits; `VirtualROTRIW` rotates by min(32, tz(imm)) (`trace_owner.cpp:381–385`).
+
+**pc and `instr_idx`.** pc is computed, never stored per row; `instr_idx` increments by 1 on every row that continues sequentially, because the committed bytecode lays each expansion out contiguously in program order (`isa/virtual.cpp:1405–1420`). The **new pc** of a row:
+
+| row | new pc |
+|---|---|
+| not its expansion's last row | pc (unchanged) |
+| last row, no control transfer | pc + `pc_increment` |
+| conditional branch | pc + imm if the comparator says taken, else as above |
+| JAL | pc + imm; **0 if imm = 0 and `imm0_pc0` is set**: Jolt's program-end convention (`trace_owner.cpp:281`) |
+| JALR | (rs1 + imm) & ~1 (`trace_owner.cpp:287`); MRET and traps are JALR rows through mepc (v36) or mtvec (v34) (`isa/virtual.hpp:36–38`; `isa/virtual.cpp:1098–1162`) |
+| `hold` set | pc (unchanged), and `instr_idx` unchanged |
+
+Then, as `finish_instruction` does (`trace_owner.cpp:111–126`): **if the new pc differs from pc,** the row transfers control: pc takes the new pc and the next `instr_idx` is the row's log word (`next_from_value` must be set; a new pc of 0 has the log word 0). **Otherwise** pc += `pc_increment` and `instr_idx` += 1, with no log word, **even for a taken branch or a jump whose target is its own pc** (`trace_owner.cpp:117–124`): Jolt treats a jump to itself as sequential, so the row's pc advances past it.
+
+**Hardwired Jolt behaviour** *(Proposed, item 57)*. Three conventions can't be table data, because they depend on values only known while the row runs; the replayer builds them in, and a change in Jolt would change silicon:
+- **the self-jump compare:** a control transfer whose new pc equals its pc is sequential (above);
+- **register 0** reads 0 and drops writes (`trace_owner.cpp:85–93`);
+- **the row record's memory fields** are formed for LD and SD only, the normalized ISA's only memory opcodes (`trace_owner.hpp:219–235`), and JALR clears bit 0 of its target (`trace_owner.cpp:287`).
+The ALU's per-opcode arithmetic is hardwired too, by nature: a new Jolt opcode needs new logic. Which rows can transfer control is explicit, not inferred from the row opcode: `next_from_value` is set in the ROM for branches, JAL and JALR, and in the controller's words for the JALR rows of MRET, ECALL and trap returns. No pc-to-index table is needed.
+
+**Rate.** About 3.3 cycles per row on average with about 10 % multiply rows (item 37 measures the density), so a 64K-row pass is about 216K cycles plus about 1.3K cycles of controller-fed expansion stalls: about 0.22 ms at 1 GHz with everything local, or about 10 ms for the 46 passes of an Akita proof [estimate].
+
+#### 4.17.8 The queues, their mode-8 ports and credits
+
+Three queues feed the replayer. They are **true FIFOs**: producers push at the tail and the replayer pops, with no slot addresses (decided 2026-10-23).
+
+| queue | size | entries | half (the refill unit) | normal source |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| code | 4 KB | 1,024 × 32-bit opcode words | 512 | always local |
+| expansion | 4 KB | 256 × 128-bit expansion words | 128 | always local |
+| value | 8 KB | 1,024 × 64-bit log words | 512 | local, or LPDDR on overflow (4.17.13) |
+
+All three are dual-ported, so pushes land while the replayer runs. Code and expansion data are always resident, so a half covers the local refill latency (a few hundred cycles) with room to spare; values may come from LPDDR, so their queue is the larger one.
+
+**The ports.** In mode 8 an address window names a queue (4.17.10): the packet is appended at the tail and the rest of its address is ignored, so the address generator's per-packet stepping stays inside the port. A packet pushes its entries low first: a 128-bit packet pushes 4 opcode words (payload [31:0] first), 2 log words ([63:0] first) or 1 expansion word; a 64-bit packet 2 opcode words or 1 log word; a 32-bit packet 1 opcode word. A size that isn't a whole number of the queue's entries is a bad replayer write *(Proposed, item 45)*.
+
+**Credits.** Each queue tracks its **occupancy** (entries landed and not yet popped) and its **pending** count (entries promised but not landed). Its **low-water event** (`WAKE_EVENTS` [16] code, [17] value, [18] expansion; 4.17.9) is a live condition, true while the replayer runs and
+
+  capacity − occupancy − pending ≥ half,
+
+and, for the expansion queue only, while the current controller-fed expansion still has words that nobody has asked for: the newest landed word's `seq_remaining` (the words after it) exceeds pending *(Proposed, item 51: how "still has words to deliver" is judged)*.
+- **A read of `WAKE_EVENTS` that returns a low-water event under `WAKE_MASK` adds half to that queue's pending,** which drops the condition. Each landed entry moves one entry from pending to occupancy (pending stops at 0). A read with the event's mask bit clear (a peek) changes nothing (4.17.9).
+- **Up to two refills can be outstanding,** which keeps a draining queue fed; a queue that empties simply stalls the replayer until data lands.
+- **The controller sends min(half, words remaining).** A short final push overstates pending, which only suppresses requests that aren't needed; the count resets at the next expansion or pass. It may also deliver a half as several smaller chunks; the queue doesn't care.
+- **A new expansion** (4.17.9): consuming the new-expansion event credits the expansion queue's first 128 words (pending = 128), so the controller sends min(128, the expansion's words from the start offset) at once, before any word has landed to judge the low-water condition by.
+
+**Overrun.** A push into a full queue (occupancy at capacity before the cycle's pop, as for every FIFO, 4.13) is dropped and sets **queue overrun** (`TILE_CONDITIONS` [12]).
+
+**Reset.** A queue's contents, occupancy and pending are cleared **at done** (the end of a pass, which may leave words a following tile needs, such as the rest of an expansion that straddles tiles), **in HALT** and **when the controller halts** (4.17.11). The expansion queue's pending is also cleared at each new expansion. **A start does not clear the queues,** so software may preload them while the replayer is stopped (4.17.10) *(Proposed, item 52)*.
+
+#### 4.17.9 The wake events and the controller loop
+
+**The events.** The replayer posts five events in the shared `WAKE_EVENTS` register (8.10, specified with the request bus), which wakes `WFI` through `WAKE_MASK` (4.12):
+
+| bit | event | kind |
 |---|---|---|
-| **mode 8, the replayer write** | 3.1, 4.8 | packets that load the replayer's memories. The traffic director routes every mode-8 packet to the replayer's write port, and no down-counter counts it (4.12). Its addr is in 32-bit words, and a stream or the send port steps it by 1, 2 or 4 words by size (4.2, 4.11). The address map, the sizes each region accepts, ownership while the replayer runs, the write path's latency and how a program knows its writes have landed are *to be specified with the replayer* |
-| **`TILE_PULSE` [2], replay start** | 8.6 | starting the replayer. **Start only, no resume**, as for every engine: a start while the replayer runs is ignored. What a start loads is *to be specified with the replayer* |
-| **`TILE_STATUS` [3] and [4]** | 8.2 | two replayer live bits, *to be specified with the replayer* |
-| **`TILE_CONDITIONS` [13:11]** | 4.13, 8.3 | three replayer conditions, *to be specified with the replayer* |
-| **registers 0x844 onward** | 8.1 | the replayer's registers, *to be specified with the replayer* |
-| **the replayer wake** | 4.12, 6.3 | one wake source in the `WFI` term, beside GO and the local wake; its events are *to be specified with the replayer* |
-
-**Until the replayer is specified** *(Proposed)*: the replayer bits of `TILE_STATUS` and `TILE_CONDITIONS` read 0; `replayer_wake` is 0; `TILE_PULSE` [2] does nothing (meshsim flags a 1 there, as for [3]); offsets 0x844 onward are unmapped, so an access is a bus error; and a mode-8 packet reaches the replayer's port and is dropped there, uncounted, with no condition (meshsim records it).
-
-**What the hooks require of any replayer design** *(Proposed)*:
-- the `done` gate blocks the replayer wake as it blocks the local wake, so `wait_for_command()` is still woken by GO alone (4.12);
-- HALT holds the replayer in reset, like the address generator and the GATHER engine (6.1), and a controller halted by an error, `ecall` or `ebreak` stops it;
-- its live bits read 0 while it is idle, so a program that never starts it sees v1.0's behaviour.
+| [16] | code low-water | live (4.17.8) |
+| [17] | value low-water | live (4.17.8) |
+| [18] | expansion low-water | live (4.17.8) |
+| [19] | new expansion: the replayer has reached an instruction whose decode class is controller (1 or 2), holds it in `REPLAYER_OPCODE` and waits (`replay_wait`, `TILE_STATUS` [4]) | sticky |
+| [20] | done: the pass has ended (`replay_running` has fallen, 4.17.11) | sticky |
+
+A load of `WAKE_EVENTS` returns every pending event and **consumes** only those under `WAKE_MASK`: a consumed low-water event credits its queue (4.17.8); a consumed new-expansion event clears and credits the first 128 expansion words; a consumed done event clears. **A read with a bit's mask clear is a peek** and changes nothing for that bit, so credits move only on a read that returns the event under the mask *(Proposed, item 50)*. A sticky event the replayer sets in the same cycle as the read survives to the next read. Software acts on value & mask. Nothing but the tile's own controller reads the register.
+
+**What the controller reads with them.** `REPLAYER_OPCODE` (8.11) holds the instruction word of the expansion it must supply (the stored opcode-stream word, bit 0 clear for a compressed instruction). `REPLAYER_STATUS` (8.11) gives the rows run in this pass and the **next offset**: the row within the current expansion where the next row starts, which is where the controller begins sending the expansion's words (non-zero only for a tile that starts mid-expansion).
 
-**Design direction** *(Open; superseded in detail)*. The owner's current replayer design is recorded in the design notes *Replayer and mesh v1.1* (updated 2026-10-23): a small ALU with fixed 3-cycle (simple) and 5-cycle (multiply) rows; code, expansion and value queues of 4, 4 and 8 KB with credit-managed refills; an 8 KB template ROM and a 2K × 16-bit decode table; the compressed trace resident in a 512 KB region of big SRAM; and a request bus to LPDDR. About 32.5 KB of replayer memory per tile replaces the 768 KB of resident replay memory planned earlier. The full replayer is to be specified in a later pass that follows that note's shared-resource allocation table.
+**The controller loop.** Every `WFI` has exactly one possible source. `REPLAY` below is `WAKE_MASK`'s replayer bits, [20:16]; the loop sets and clears them with read-modify-write, leaving the request bus's bits alone.
 
-The replayer's memories and logic are not in section 11's budget; they are added with the replayer. Under the design direction they come to about 32.5 KB of memory per tile plus a small ALU [estimate].
+```
+    WAKE_MASK |= REPLAY
+    TILE_PULSE = replay start
+loop:
+    WFI                              ; only a replayer event can wake it
+    ev = WAKE_EVENTS & REPLAY        ; consumes the events: credits taken
+    if ev.done: exit
+    if ev.new_expansion: decode REPLAYER_OPCODE (inlines and system only),
+                         start at REPLAYER_STATUS.next_offset
+    WAKE_MASK &= ~REPLAY             ; squelch: events now accumulate
+    set DMA_REPLAYER_REMAINING; write STREAM entries; start the run
+    WFI                              ; only the counter reaching 0 can wake it
+    WAKE_MASK |= REPLAY              ; events posted meanwhile fall through
+    goto loop
+```
+
+The wake term is `GO | local_latch | (WAKE_EVENTS & WAKE_MASK) ≠ 0` (4.12). `DMA_REPLAYER_REMAINING` counts the run's mode-8 packets as they land (4.17.10), so the second `WFI` returns when every word is in its queue. Events posted during a run accumulate and, the wake being a level, make the next `WFI` fall through. GO can't arrive mid-pass, and no other counter is set. A refill from LPDDR (4.17.13) is a request instead of a run, and its completion counts in `DMA_REPLY_REMAINING` (4.12).
+
+**Short runs, not interruption.** The address generator is not interruptible. Instead every run is short: a 1 KB chunk is 64 packets, about 64 cycles, so nothing waits long for the engine, and the controller re-checks the queues after each chunk. Streaming occupies about 11–20 % of a pass [estimate]. Group runs (the sort's all-to-alls) stay exclusive and don't coincide with replay.
+
+**Controller-fed expansions** are only the inlines (custom-0) and ECALL, EBREAK, MRET, CSRRW and CSRRS: about 13 per 64K-row window on Cyclone, about 0.4 % of a pass in stalls [meas: design notes]. CSRRW and CSRRS expand to 1–3 rows depending on their registers (`num_cycles`, `isa/virtual.cpp:1324–1345`), which is one reason they stay with the controller. The current window's inline templates are resident in big SRAM (4.17.13).
+
+#### 4.17.10 Mode 8: the address map, landing and ownership
+
+**The map.** Everything the replayer reads is written by mode 8, whose addr is a 21-bit word address stepped by 1, 2 or 4 per packet (3.1, 4.2, 4.11). addr[20:18] selects a window *(Proposed, item 44)*:
+
+| addr (32-bit words) | addr[20:18] | region | size | sizes taken | ports |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 0x00000–0x000FF | 0 | register image: 128-bit word *i* at 4*i* | 1 KB | 128 | single |
+| 0x00100–0x00103 | 0 | state word | 16 B | 128 | single |
+| 0x00400–0x007FF | 0 | decode table: entry *e* in the packet at 0x400 + 4·(*e* >> 3), bits [16·(*e* & 7) + 15 : 16·(*e* & 7)] | 4 KB | 128 | single |
+| 0x00800–0x00FFF | 0 | ROM: word *j* at 0x800 + 4*j* | 8 KB | 128 | dual |
+| 0x40000–0x7FFFF | 1 | code queue port | 4 KB queue | 32, 64, 128 | dual |
+| 0x80000–0xBFFFF | 2 | value queue port | 8 KB queue | 64, 128 | dual |
+| 0xC0000–0xFFFFF | 3 | expansion queue port | 4 KB queue | 128 | dual |
+| everything else | 0, 4–7 | unmapped | — | — | — |
+
+*(Proposed, items 44–46: the windows, the offsets in window 0 and the sizes each region takes.)* The notes' map is in 128-bit words (packet addr = 4 ×); in those units the register image is 0–63, the state word 64, the decode table 256–511 and the ROM 512–1023. A 128-bit write in window 0 must start at a multiple of 4. The working register file (1 KB, true dual-port) isn't addressable; each start fills it from the image.
+
+**A bad replayer write** (`TILE_CONDITIONS` [11]) is a mode-8 packet that is dropped because it addresses an unmapped word, has a size its region doesn't take, starts a 128-bit write in window 0 off a multiple of 4, writes a single-ported memory while a pass runs, or writes a queue port in HALT (below) *(Proposed, item 47)*.
+
+**Landing.** The traffic director hands each mode-8 packet to the replayer's write port, one per cycle; the write lands a fixed **`rp_latency`** = 2 cycles later, with no queue on the path *(Proposed, item 48: the value)*.
+
+**Counting** (the allocation table, which supersedes v1.1's "mode 8 is never counted"). A mode-8 packet **from the tile itself** (src = the tile's index) counts 1 in **`DMA_REPLAYER_REMAINING`** (8.11) **when it lands**, or when it is dropped as a bad replayer write or a queue overrun, so a count of 0 means every counted write has landed or been refused and no idle bit is needed *(Proposed, item 48)*. Packets from C&C (the memory system's queue refills, boot loads) are uncounted, like C&C's other data (4.12); a requested transfer proves its landing with its mode-7 completion, which follows its data on the same route. A mode-8 packet from another tile is uncounted and is a programming error the simulator flags (invariant 16) *(Proposed, item 49)*. An arrival for the counter at 0 sets unexpected arrival (4.12).
+
+**Ownership** follows `replay_running` and the RUN/HALT line:
+
+| state | image, state word, decode table | ROM | queue ports |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **stopped** (in RUN, `replay_running` = 0) | written | written | pushed (preload) |
+| **running** (`replay_running` = 1) | bad replayer write | written (second port) | pushed |
+| **HALT** | written (boot loads) | written | bad replayer write |
+
+*(Proposed, item 54: the HALT row; the notes give the first two.)* A halt hands everything back.
+
+#### 4.17.11 Start, done, halt and the conditions
+
+**`TILE_PULSE` [2], replay start.** Sets `replay_running` (`TILE_STATUS` [3]), giving the replayer its single-ported memories until it finishes or halts; copies the register image into the working file (64 cycles) and the state word into working pc, `instr_idx` and offset; clears the rows-run count. The first row starts when the copy is done. **Start only, no resume,** as for every engine: a start while the replayer runs is ignored (meshsim flags it, 4.13). 
+
+**`TILE_STATUS` [3] `replay_running`, [4] `replay_wait`.** `replay_running` is 1 from the end of the start store's cycle until the pass's last row has been pushed into the output queue, or a halt. `replay_wait` is 1 while the replayer waits for the first word of a controller-fed expansion: set with the new-expansion event, cleared when that word lands *(Proposed, item 53: when [4] clears)*. Both read 0 while the replayer is idle, so a program that never starts it sees v1.0's behaviour.
+
+**Done.** The pass ends after its **65,536th row**: `replay_running` falls, the done event ([20]) is posted, and the queues reset (4.17.8). Rows still in the output queue drain to the consumer. Done is `replay_running` = 0.
+
+**Halt.** C&C's HALT, or the controller halting itself (bus error, illegal instruction, `ecall`, `ebreak`; 6.1), stops the replayer at once: `replay_running` and `replay_wait` drop, its queues, its events and its stop state reset, and it stays idle until the next start. HALT holds it in reset. Its memories keep their contents and, in HALT, take mode-8 writes (4.17.10).
+
+**The conditions** (raise and run, as in v1.0):
+
+| bit | condition | set when | kind |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 11 | bad replayer write | a mode-8 packet was dropped (4.17.10) | P |
+| 12 | queue overrun | a push found its queue full and was dropped (4.17.8) | P |
+| 13 | invalid expansion | a decode-table class 3; an expansion word with a reserved operand code (145–255), `format` 6 or 7, or an opcode the replayer doesn't execute (4.17.7); or a template running past ROM word 511 (4.17.6) | P, and H on a corrupted table or ROM |
+
+*(Proposed, item 55: the kinds, and the opcode check.)* **An invalid expansion stops the pass** as if it had ended: `replay_running` falls and the done event is posted, so the controller loop exits instead of waiting for rows that can't be right; the condition says why *(Proposed, item 55)*. The other two conditions leave the replayer running.
+
+#### 4.17.12 The output row
+
+**The record** *(Proposed, item 59: the layout; the field semantics are Jolt's)*. Each row is 640 bits, five 128-bit words, carrying what Jolt's 80-byte trace row `RiscCycle` carries (`trace_owner.hpp:19–94`) plus `instr_idx`, which Jolt's provers take from a separate column (`trace_columns.inc:53`), and less the memory post-value, which the consumer derives (an LD's is the value loaded, an SD's is rs2's value; `trace_owner.hpp:230–235`). The values are those `expand_compressed_chunk` records (`trace_owner.cpp:893–927`):
+
+| word | bits | field | value |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 0 | [31:0] | `instr_idx` | the row's bytecode index (0 in padding) |
+| 0 | [39:32] | `opcode` | the row opcode; `VirtualAdvice` (122) for every advice row (4.17.7) |
+| 0 | [47:40] | `rd_reg` | rd's register number, 0 if rd is an immediate slot |
+| 0 | [55:48] | `rs1_reg` | rs1's register number, 0 if an immediate slot |
+| 0 | [63:56] | `rs2_reg` | rs2's register number, 0 if an immediate slot |
+| 0 | [79:64] | `inline_sequence_remaining` | `seq_remaining` |
+| 0 | [80] | `is_compressed` | 4.17.3 |
+| 0 | [81] | `is_virtual` | 4.17.3 |
+| 0 | [82] | `is_first_in_sequence` | 4.17.3 |
+| 0 | [85:83] | `rd_is_imm`, `rs1_is_imm`, `rs2_is_imm` | the slot kinds (4.17.3), as the bytecode commits them |
+| 0 | [127:86] | spare | 0 |
+| 1 | [63:0] | `pc` | the source instruction's pc (0 in padding) |
+| 1 | [127:64] | `imm` | the sum of the immediate slots (4.17.3); Jolt's raw `_imm`, which consumers normalize by opcode (`imm()`, `trace_owner.hpp:80–92`) |
+| 2 | [63:0] | `rs1` | rs1's value before the row; 0 if an immediate slot |
+| 2 | [127:64] | `rs2` | rs2's value before the row; 0 if an immediate slot |
+| 3 | [63:0] | `rd_prev` | rd's value before the row; 0 if an immediate slot |
+| 3 | [127:64] | `rd` | rd's value after the row; 0 if an immediate slot, and 0 for x0 |
+| 4 | [63:0] | `mem_addr` | rs1 + imm for LD and SD, else 0 |
+| 4 | [127:64] | `mem_value` | the value loaded (LD), the memory pre-value from the log (SD), else 0 |
+
+A padding row's record is all zeros, as Jolt's padded rows are (4.17.2).
+
+**The output queue** holds 32 rows (about 2.5 KB) between the replayer and its consumer, with ready/valid backpressure: when it is full, the replayer stops before its next row. It absorbs consumer slowdowns and the replayer's own stalls on expansion misses (tens of cycles). The consumer's side of it, its width and its rate, belong to the materializer and are *(Open, item 59)*; until it is specified, meshsim drains it with a sink that takes one row per cycle.
+
+#### 4.17.13 The resident replay region (the software contract)
+
+The trace stays in big SRAM for the whole proof, in a **512 KB replay region** per tile, a software-managed cache. The hardware knows nothing of it: it sees only the mode-8 packets the controller's streams and the memory system send. This subsection is the contract software keeps so that the hardware above works.
+
+**Layout.**
+1. **This window's templates first:** only the controller-fed templates its 64K rows actually use (often none; a window of BLAKE3 calls needs about 20 KB [estimate]). The tracer engine lists the set with the checkpoint.
+2. **Then code and values** as 4 KB blocks in consumption order, opcode and value blocks interleaved, so "resident" is a single boundary.
+3. **Overflow** beyond 512 KB, at most about 190 KB in the densest windows [meas: design notes], stays in LPDDR and is fetched over the request bus in 4 KB requests as the replayer reaches it, delivered straight to the value or code queue port.
+
+The median window's trace is about 335 KB and the densest about 700 KB [meas: design notes]. At the stages whose scratchpad margin is too small, the controller evicts the region and reloads it afterwards (up to 512 KB per tile, about 33 MB per chip, about 2 ms [estimate]). The same mechanism can spill compact, expensive-to-rebuild intermediates; anything the trace can regenerate is better replayed (about 0.2 ms per pass) than spilled. In sumcheck tails the controller can prefetch the next pass's streams from LPDDR (about 21 MB per chip, about 1.3 ms at 16–17 GB/s [estimate]), into areas the remaining rounds don't use, with C&C pacing the fill so it never fans in.
+
+**The rules software keeps:**
+- **Stream to a queue port from its window's base** (4.17.10), with runs of at most a half; a refill is min(half, words remaining) (4.17.8).
+- **At most one outstanding LPDDR read per queue,** because the memory system may answer reads out of order except into one queue; local refills may have two outstanding (4.17.8).
+- **Only the tile itself and C&C send mode 8** (invariant 16).
+- **Load the checkpoint, the decode table and the ROM while the replayer is stopped or the tile is halted** (4.17.10); preload queues only while it is stopped, never in HALT.
+- **Don't send past a pass's needs:** words that land after done are taken as the next pass's preload.
+
+**Code is not in big SRAM.** Each tile controller has its own instruction and data memories, and stage overlays are broadcast by C&C at stage boundaries into a parked controller (6.5), so the earlier plan's 256 KiB code reservation in big SRAM is reclaimed for data.
+
+#### 4.17.14 Cost
+
+**Memory per tile:** 4 + 4 + 8 KB of queues, an 8 KB ROM, a 4 KB decode table, 1 KB of working registers, about 1 KB of image and state, and about 2.5 KB of output queue (32 × 80 B): **about 32.5 KB** [estimate]. Across 64 tiles that is about 2.2 MB, roughly 7–10 mm² per chip with the logic [estimate], against 48 MB for the 768 KB-per-tile design planned earlier.
+
+**Logic per tile:** the ALU and comparator, the 128 × 64-bit true dual-port register file, two adders (memory address, pc + imm), the operand resolver, the decode index logic, three queue readers and the credit counters: about 0.1–0.15 mm² with its memories [estimate]. Section 11 adds it to the tile's budget; its placement is in section 12.
 
